<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gottlob Frege</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich Ludwig Gottlob Frege</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gottlob Frege was a German philosopher, logician and mathematician who taught mathematics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> and is regarded by many as the father of analytic philosophy. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a>, he developed modern logic in his Begriffsschrift of 1879 and argued in The Foundations of Arithmetic (1884) that arithmetic is a branch of logic. As the second volume of his Basic Laws of Arithmetic went to press in 1903, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-russell">Bertrand Russell</a> wrote to him showing that a paradox could be derived from its Basic Law V. Largely ignored in his lifetime, his work, including the paper On Sense and Reference, reached later philosophers through Peano, Russell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Wittgenstein</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gottlob-frege.jpg" alt="Gottlob Frege" width="330" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Young_frege.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
